From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 01:33:31 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: bound the command allocator storage kept between
 recordings

A Metal 4 command allocator keeps its storage at its high-water mark when
it is reset, and releasing it is the only way to return that storage. The
device cached up to 24 reset allocators with no limit on their size, and
every command buffer took an allocator for each of its three lanes when it
was created or begun and kept them across recordings, whether or not it
recorded anything. A GL driver begins spare command buffers it seldom
uses, so 150 s into a Plasma desktop session on a Mac, 255 allocators
held 3.6 GiB of command storage, the largest 1.1 GiB, while no recording
needed it.

A command buffer now takes an allocator for a lane only when that lane
opens a Metal command buffer, and hands it to the device cache when the
recording is reset, as a completed one-time recording already did. The
cache recycles only allocators of at most 16 MiB, since a recording that
takes a grown one keeps all of that storage however little it records. It
keeps at most 64 MiB in total and releases every other allocator.

With the previous commit, the same session peaks at one 257 MiB allocator
during its first frame and then keeps 39 MiB in 9 allocators, none larger
than 6 MiB. Without the 16 MiB recycling limit it kept 61 MiB, 41 MiB of
it in two allocators larger than 16 MiB.
